WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Validation failure should produce
predictable behavior.

2
00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:08.058
Fail-closed behavior means the program
rejects input and avoids unsafe continuation

3
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:09.902
when validation does not succeed.

4
00:00:09.982 --> 00:00:14.238
The code should not keep processing a
value simply because a later layer might catch

5
00:00:14.318 --> 00:00:14.986
the problem.

6
00:00:15.066 --> 00:00:17.438
Avoid partial state updates on invalid
input.

7
00:00:17.518 --> 00:00:21.530
If a parser updates configuration,
modifies an object, allocates resources,

8
00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.164
or writes records before validation is
complete,

9
00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:27.162
a failure can leave the program in an
inconsistent state.

10
00:00:27.242 --> 00:00:31.255
A safer pattern validates into temporary
state and commits only after the full

11
00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:32.551
contract is satisfied.

12
00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:35.489
Temporary resources should be cleaned up
on rejection.

13
00:00:35.569 --> 00:00:38.487
Buffers, parsed fields, dynamic objects,
file handles,

14
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:42.398
and intermediate records should have clear
ownership during validation.

15
00:00:42.478 --> 00:00:45.761
Failure paths deserve the same cleanup
review as success paths.

16
00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:48.030
Error reporting should be useful but safe.

17
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:51.636
Logs can record source, field name,
failure category, length range,

18
00:00:51.716 --> 00:00:55.182
or validation rule without copying
secrets, raw sensitive values,

19
00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:56.538
or large untrusted text.

20
00:00:56.618 --> 00:01:00.996
User-facing messages should avoid
unnecessary internal details while still making

21
00:01:01.076 --> 00:01:04.055
the failure understandable enough for
normal operation.

22
00:01:04.135 --> 00:01:08.391
Most importantly, invalid data should not
continue deeper into the program under a

23
00:01:08.471 --> 00:01:11.937
warning-only model unless the contract
explicitly supports that.

24
00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:16.151
Once validation fails, later code should
not have to defend against the rejected

25
00:01:16.231 --> 00:01:18.420
representation as if it were still usable.
